In basket trials where patients with various cancer types are treated equally, 
a one-size-fits-all approach can be both beneficial and problematic. Although 
sharing data across different subsets is useful, it also risks diluting treatment 
effects in individual subtypes. Our research develops a Bayesian method that 
estimates the magnitude of the treatment effect in each subtype while also 
considering the potential benefits of borrowing information from others. This 
approach measures the uncertainty of the estimate by using the predictive 
predictive distribution and compares it to a scenario where no data is shared. Our 
results indicate that the Bayesian approach is more accurate than a frequentist 
method that only compares the means of subtypes. Furthermore, we demonstrate 
that the Bayesian method is more efficient by reducing the required sample size 
needed to achieve the same level of precision as a frequentist treatment. By 
evaluating our approach on a real-world basket trial, we show that the Bayesian 
method can provide more precise results and accurately classify new patients 
without the need for additional data. Finally, we outline two possible ways to 
extend our method to assess the homogeneity of subtypes and the likelihood of a 
positive treatment effect in future trials. 

